  "metric_definitions": {
    "error_e3d": "Mean normalized 3D reconstruction error; dimensionless; lower is better. Shapes are registered to ground truth.",
    "error_eR": "Mean Frobenius-norm rotation error; lower is better. Not angular degrees.",
    "rmse_mm": "3D reconstruction RMSE in millimeters, averaged over six camera trajectories; lower is better.",
    "runtime_seconds": "Wall-clock seconds for the specified stage or overall solver; lower is better."
  },
  "timing_scope_note": "The main paper uses Apple M3 Max unless otherwise noted; dense experiments requiring CUDA SVD use NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada. Released implementations/backends and hardware vary when identical-hardware reruns were unavailable; runtimes are reported measurements/scaling indicators, not normalized same-hardware estimates.",
  "dense_scope_note": "Table 5 caption says overall run-time. Its Ours values exactly equal rotation+shape totals in supplement Table 14. Main p6 uses the broader wording dense shape-space solver runtime. Use overall/rotation+shape for these four Ours values; do not describe them as shape-stage-only.",
  "null_note": "Dense methods other than OPM, TSM, and Ours lack solver runtime measurements. ND has no Seq.1/2 error values; Seq.3/4 show 3h+ train, which is not inference/solver runtime and is not numeric chart data.",
  "rotation_tsm_note": "Table 2 gives two TSM errors: BMM-initialized R_init (stored in error_eR) and refined R_ref (tsm_refined_error_eR); only one runtime is reported per sequence. Do not silently choose/average the errors.",
  "scaling_note": "F and P are sequence metadata, not controlled frame-count/point-count sweeps. No sweep values were invented.",
